Time the physics and the replay write separately.

`play_loop_timer` is where a frame's time went - about 2.2 seconds of it - and the two
things inside it are opposite problems. `game_server_step` is arithmetic, and slow
arithmetic means the solver needs work. `game_client_sync` writes the replay through
`demo_fp`, and slow I/O means the stream underneath needs a buffer.

Guessing between them cost several hardware runs; four clock reads settled it in one:

    play: 1 steps, server 0ms, sync 2304ms, demo on

The solver was instant and had been all along, and every millisecond was the replay - each
command writing its shorts and floats as separate two-to-four-byte calls into a libc whose
`fwrite` went straight to a syscall. Buffering that stream in the SDK took the same line to
`server 4ms, sync 2ms` across thirty frames a second.

It stays because it is also a frame-rate readout, and a port wants one.

diff --git a/ball/st_play.c b/ball/st_play.c
index bd5323a..030dd5e 100644
--- a/ball/st_play.c
+++ b/ball/st_play.c
@@ -12,6 +12,8 @@
